1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,855
Copper wires run through every home.

2
00:00:02,855 --> 00:00:04,751
Chromium coats a shiny tap.

3
00:00:04,751 --> 00:00:06,547
Iron makes a cooking pan.

4
00:00:06,947 --> 00:00:10,567
Three metals, almost side by
side in the periodic table.

5
00:00:10,567 --> 00:00:12,614
And two of them break a rule.

6
00:00:13,064 --> 00:00:15,291
Diya notices them in her kitchen.

7
00:00:15,291 --> 00:00:18,946
A copper bottomed pot, a
shiny tap, and an iron pan.

8
00:00:19,296 --> 00:00:22,140
She writes their electrons
with the filling rules.

9
00:00:22,140 --> 00:00:25,979
But for chromium and copper, the
textbook gives a different answer.

10
00:00:26,479 --> 00:00:30,498
By the end of this video, you will
write the configuration of any element

11
00:00:30,498 --> 00:00:33,357
up to zinc, and know why
these two are special.

12
00:00:34,007 --> 00:00:37,038
First, what is an
electronic configuration?

13
00:00:37,388 --> 00:00:41,680
It is the way the electrons of an
atom are spread over its orbitals.

14
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:44,522
We write it in s p d f notation.

15
00:00:44,522 --> 00:00:45,513
Take sodium.

16
00:00:45,513 --> 00:00:49,110
One s two, two s two,
two p six, three s one.

17
00:00:49,110 --> 00:00:53,739
The small number on top tells how
many electrons sit in that subshell.

18
00:00:54,339 --> 00:00:56,508
Or we draw an orbital diagram.

19
00:00:56,508 --> 00:01:00,323
Each box is an orbital, and
each arrow is an electron.

20
00:01:00,923 --> 00:01:05,145
Let's build atoms on our molecule
bench, one electron at a time.

21
00:01:05,495 --> 00:01:08,192
Hydrogen has one electron, in one s.

22
00:01:08,192 --> 00:01:09,430
Helium has two.

23
00:01:09,430 --> 00:01:12,400
Lithium starts the next shell, in two s.

24
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:15,539
From boron to neon, two p fills up.

25
00:01:15,539 --> 00:01:17,725
Neon has a full second shell.

26
00:01:17,725 --> 00:01:20,475
One s two, two s two, two p six.

27
00:01:20,875 --> 00:01:23,776
Sodium to argon fill three s and three p.

28
00:01:23,776 --> 00:01:26,289
The same pattern, one shell higher.

29
00:01:26,689 --> 00:01:31,530
Then potassium and calcium put their
new electrons in four s, because four

30
00:01:31,530 --> 00:01:34,071
s is lower in energy than three d.

31
00:01:34,471 --> 00:01:37,123
From scandium, three d starts to fill.

32
00:01:37,123 --> 00:01:40,242
Scandium is argon,
three d one, four s two.

33
00:01:40,642 --> 00:01:45,313
To save space, we write the core
as a noble gas in square brackets.

34
00:01:45,313 --> 00:01:48,791
Argon in brackets means the
first eighteen electrons.

35
00:01:49,441 --> 00:01:51,664
Now chromium, number twenty four.

36
00:01:51,664 --> 00:01:55,097
The rules predict argon,
three d four, four s two.

37
00:01:55,497 --> 00:01:59,094
But one electron moves up
from four s into three d.

38
00:01:59,094 --> 00:02:02,410
Chromium is argon, three
d five, four s one.

39
00:02:02,810 --> 00:02:05,716
Copper, number twenty nine, does the same.

40
00:02:05,716 --> 00:02:09,913
Not three d nine, four s two,
but three d ten, four s one.

41
00:02:10,313 --> 00:02:13,313
Four s and three d are
very close in energy.

42
00:02:13,313 --> 00:02:18,123
So the shift happens when it leaves three
d exactly half filled, or completely

43
00:02:18,123 --> 00:02:19,023
filled.

44
00:02:19,422 --> 00:02:22,615
Why are half filled and
full subshells extra stable?

45
00:02:22,615 --> 00:02:24,112
There are two reasons.

46
00:02:24,512 --> 00:02:25,609
One, symmetry.

47
00:02:25,609 --> 00:02:29,949
The electrons are spread evenly, so
they shield each other less, and the

48
00:02:29,949 --> 00:02:32,011
nucleus holds them more strongly.

49
00:02:32,411 --> 00:02:34,021
Two, exchange energy.

50
00:02:34,021 --> 00:02:38,749
Electrons with the same spin in a
subshell can swap places, and each swap

51
00:02:38,749 --> 00:02:39,941
releases energy.

52
00:02:39,941 --> 00:02:43,395
Half filled and full subshells
allow the most swaps.

53
00:02:44,045 --> 00:02:46,705
Look at two configurations for copper.

54
00:02:46,705 --> 00:02:49,323
Argon, three d nine, four s two.

55
00:02:49,323 --> 00:02:52,163
And argon, three d ten, four s one.

56
00:02:52,563 --> 00:02:53,739
Pause and think.

57
00:02:53,739 --> 00:02:56,054
Which one is the real ground state?

58
00:02:59,254 --> 00:03:00,257
The second.

59
00:03:00,257 --> 00:03:05,321
A completely filled three d is extra
stable, so one four s electron moves

60
00:03:05,321 --> 00:03:06,499
into three d.

61
00:03:07,149 --> 00:03:10,928
Let's count the swaps in three
d five, one step at a time.

62
00:03:11,278 --> 00:03:12,178
Step one.

63
00:03:12,112 --> 00:03:15,885
The first electron can swap
with each of the other four.

64
00:03:15,885 --> 00:03:17,553
That is four exchanges.

65
00:03:18,303 --> 00:03:19,203
Step two.

66
00:03:19,156 --> 00:03:22,090
The second electron
has three new partners.

67
00:03:22,090 --> 00:03:23,048
Three more.

68
00:03:23,798 --> 00:03:24,698
Step three.

69
00:03:24,666 --> 00:03:26,366
Then two, and then one.

70
00:03:27,116 --> 00:03:28,068
Step four.

71
00:03:28,068 --> 00:03:31,176
Four plus three plus two plus one is ten.

72
00:03:31,176 --> 00:03:33,431
Three d four allows only six.

73
00:03:33,431 --> 00:03:35,182
So three d five wins.

74
00:03:35,932 --> 00:03:37,032
Remember this.

75
00:03:37,032 --> 00:03:40,982
Half filled and completely filled
subshells are extra stable.

76
00:03:41,682 --> 00:03:43,418
Now, one worked example.

77
00:03:43,718 --> 00:03:48,456
Write the configuration of iron, number
twenty six, and count its unpaired

78
00:03:48,456 --> 00:03:49,356
electrons.

79
00:03:49,778 --> 00:03:51,244
Argon takes eighteen.

80
00:03:51,244 --> 00:03:52,562
That leaves eight.

81
00:03:53,312 --> 00:03:56,447
Four s takes two, and six go into three d.

82
00:03:56,447 --> 00:03:59,469
Iron is argon, three d six, four s two.

83
00:04:00,219 --> 00:04:03,796
In three d, the first five
electrons go one per box.

84
00:04:03,796 --> 00:04:06,302
The sixth pairs up in the first box.

85
00:04:07,052 --> 00:04:09,664
So iron has four unpaired electrons.

86
00:04:10,314 --> 00:04:11,779
Now, the exam trap.

87
00:04:12,129 --> 00:04:16,864
Many students follow the rules blindly,
and write chromium as argon, three

88
00:04:16,864 --> 00:04:18,487
d four, four s two.

89
00:04:18,887 --> 00:04:22,314
But chromium is argon,
three d five, four s one.

90
00:04:22,314 --> 00:04:24,878
A half filled three d is more stable.

91
00:04:24,878 --> 00:04:27,987
Learn chromium and copper
as the two exceptions.

92
00:04:28,637 --> 00:04:30,210
Where is this asked?

93
00:04:30,210 --> 00:04:35,087
In JEE Main and NEET, usually as
unpaired electrons, or the configuration

94
00:04:35,087 --> 00:04:36,818
of chromium and copper.

95
00:04:37,418 --> 00:04:38,657
Exam level now.

96
00:04:38,657 --> 00:04:43,631
In a ground state copper atom, how
many electrons have l equal to zero?

97
00:04:43,631 --> 00:04:44,922
Pause and solve.

98
00:04:50,322 --> 00:04:51,512
The answer is B.

99
00:04:51,512 --> 00:04:52,412
Seven.

100
00:04:52,574 --> 00:04:55,275
l equal to zero means s electrons.

101
00:04:55,275 --> 00:04:58,740
Copper is argon, three d ten, four s one.

102
00:04:59,240 --> 00:05:00,789
Count every s subshell.

103
00:05:00,789 --> 00:05:04,407
One s two, two s two, three
s two, and four s one.

104
00:05:04,907 --> 00:05:07,768
Two plus two plus two plus one is seven.

105
00:05:07,768 --> 00:05:12,619
Option C, eight, forgot that copper
keeps only one electron in four s.

106
00:05:13,219 --> 00:05:14,678
Here is your recap.

107
00:05:14,678 --> 00:05:16,177
Screenshot this card.

108
00:05:19,377 --> 00:05:22,376
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

109
00:05:22,776 --> 00:05:23,676
One.

110
00:05:23,313 --> 00:05:25,788
Write the configuration of chromium.

111
00:05:26,588 --> 00:05:27,488
Two.

112
00:05:27,141 --> 00:05:30,789
Why is three d ten more
stable than three d nine?

113
00:05:31,589 --> 00:05:32,489
Three.

114
00:05:32,221 --> 00:05:35,142
How many unpaired
electrons does iron have?

115
00:05:36,042 --> 00:05:40,586
Next time: how did chemists line up
the elements, long before they knew

116
00:05:40,586 --> 00:05:41,747
about electrons?

117
00:05:41,747 --> 00:05:45,150
We find out in development
of periodic classification.
